<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elwell Otis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a>–<a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Elwell Stephen Otis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elwell Otis was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army general who served as military governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from 1898 to 1900. He commanded American forces at the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a> in 1899 and repeatedly claimed the insurgency was nearly over. He was succeeded by Arthur MacArthur in 1900.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elwell-otis.jpg" alt="Elwell Otis" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kurz &amp; Allison · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elwell_S._Otis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Otis was born in Frederick, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, on 25 March 1838. He attended the University of Rochester, graduated from Harvard Law School in 1860 and was practising law in the first year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. In September 1862 he was appointed captain in the 140th New York Volunteer Infantry Regiment, raised in Rochester, and fought at Fredericksburg, Chancellorsville and Gettysburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Promoted lieutenant colonel in December 1863, Otis took command of his regiment when its colonel was killed at the Battle of Spotsylvania and fought through the Overland Campaign. During the Siege of Petersburg he led a brigade of V Corps at the Battle of Peebles' Farm, where a severe wound ended his field service in the war. He became lieutenant colonel of the 22nd U.S. Infantry in 1867, campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> after the Battle of the Little Bighorn, was made colonel of the 20th U.S. Infantry in 1880 and brigadier general in 1893.</p><p>Appointed major general of volunteers in May 1898, he took reinforcements to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and replaced General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wesley-merritt">Wesley Merritt</a> in command of the Eighth Army Corps. He directed the army in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in 1899 and in the first phase of the war before it turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> fighting. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a> sent an emissary to appeal for an end to the fighting, Otis refused, saying that fighting, having begun, must go on to the grim end.</p><p>In May 1899 the American Anti-Imperialist League published soldiers' letters describing atrocities by American troops. Otis denied any abuses to the press and threatened the writers with court-martial unless they retracted, though he admitted that he suspected the Washington Volunteers of some burning and cruelty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rochester welcomed him back on 15 June 1900 with a 50-foot arch over its two main streets, and for years marked the day as Otis Day. He later commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Lakes and became major general in the regular army in 1906. Regarded as an able administrator, he was disliked by many subordinates and peers; his troops called him Granny. He died of angina in Rochester on 21 October 1909 and was later reburied at Arlington National Cemetery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elwell Stephen Otis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Military governors, Wesley Merritt, <b>Elwell Otis</b>, Arthur MacArthur and Adna Chaffee, held executive and legislative power.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-military-government-of-the-philippine-islands">United States Military Government of the Philippine Islands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Frederick, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Rochester, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.43,-77.42&amp;year=1884" aria-label="Frederick on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2637 751 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Elwell Otis"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3087 1021h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3087 1021h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.43,-77.42&amp;year=1884">What was here in 1884? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
